Hi again,


I posted last week with an early version of a new roguelike that I
have been working on for some time, Lords of DarkHall. The file host I
chose wasn't the best, but I still got some nice feedback and advice.

That being said, I spent the rest of the week, and a lot of the
weekend refining, working out bugs, and adding new features. The
latest version can be grabbed here:

http://www.mediafire.com/?fbdyyevlhor



And the new features are summarized here:

Melee combat is in and working
Monster AI has been tweaked ('smart' monsters run at low health,
mindless ones continue to attack, etc)
A few new monster images
Autotargetting, and manual target selection (in advance of the next
release having ranged combat and magic)
Monster health bars
Monsters now drop loot items
Monsters now spawn in the levels over time
A fairly finalized version of the speed system is in place and
completely functional
Changes to the interface
Health potions
Spaces can hold multiple objects



In the next release:
statistics on the character screen (ie HPs, dmg, chance to open locks,
find doors, etc - more transparency)
ranged combat
magic
hunger
many more monsters
possibly an overworld 'entrance' level - eg, a forest or town



For the future:

a town or overworld with shops
skills
better art than the placeholder art I have now


At this point, the game is quite playable, and if you desire, you can
tweak the monsters, or add as many as you want! Balance is still WAY
off - I am going to have to do something about the 'swarm' which
attacks you in more open levels....or simply make those levels appear
later when the player will be better able to deal with them.

Also, I am leaning towards a sort of 'arcadesy' type magic system, as
you might guess from the mana bar and autotarget features in this
release. I always hated Angband's system of manually selecting a
target, then choosing a spellbook, then a spell....Multiple keypresses
for a single action = tedium. (Yes, I know you can use macros, but I
want something EASY and fun.) My general idea is that you choose a
default ranged attack/spell, and pressing a single button (probably
space bar) will automatically shoot at the selected target. The target
system (which can be toggled) already will auto-target the closest
monster, or allow you to target a different one you can see, and will
stay 'stuck' to that target until he moves out of range, or dies. (I
have been playing around with the Castlevania RL, and, while there are
some truly odd design decisions, really enjoying the arcade feel of
the game.


Also, I want to mention some other roguelikes I am enjoying and
drawing inspiration from. It goes without saying that Nethack is my
number 1 inspiration (and maybe all-time favorite game), and of course
Angband and the other classic roguelikes. But, as I mentioned above, I
have really been enjoying Castlevania RL, as well as the other
'arcade' RLs they are developing - Metroid looks like it might be a
lot of fun - I really need to spend more time with it.

Also....Incursion.....WOW.....what a cool game. I am completely in awe
of the work the author has done on this game. It is OVERWHELMING in
sheer complexity and options available to the player. My ONLY
complaint about this game is that it may provide TOO much. And is that
really a complaint?? Seriously, you need to check out this game. It is
approaching a work of art.

If you have a free minute, please take a look and my game, and maybe
post a comment or 2 - good or bad. I am working hard on this game, and
really want to make something people will actually enjoy.

Thanks for your time!
Brigand



